Title: The Statue
Credit: written by
Author: Written by





EXT. FERTILITY CLINIC COURTYARD - DAY

A stone statue of a woman, her hands cradled as if holding a baby, yet, there is no baby. Around her, couples walk past, some are clearly pregnant.

INT. FERTLITY CLINIC DOCTOR'S OFFICE - DAY

A young couple, Margaret Williams (25) and Alexander Williams (26) sit across from an older woman, Dr. Davies.

DR. DAVIES
So, if all goes well; which it should do, my hopes are very high, all should have been successful, and...

MARGARET
Yes! We will finally be pregnant.

Margaret grabbing Alexander's hand and turning to him.

ALEXANDER
We've waited so long for this.

MARGARET
Its really happening!

DR. DAVIES
Do just remember, just because the fertilization worked, there are still risks, and we need to monitor your condition, relatively often as you both know. 

Margaret nods at the Dr Davies, and places her hand on her stomach. 

ALEXANDER
Yes of course, I just hope that we are pregnant.

INT. WILLIAM'S HOUSE LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Margaret lays on the couch, a blanket wrapped around her, a medical drama plays on the television. 

ALEXANDER (O.S.)
No we won't know for a few weeks - yes, Its a long time to wait - Yes! We are very thank full for your help mum - No we didn't think we had to pay you back - Ok, you go see Diane - I will talk to you later, bye! - Yes, I love you - bye!

Alexander comes into the living room. Margaret moves her legs, and Alexander slouches into the couch, 

MARGARET
She's good?

ALEXANDER
Excited for us. Could you not hear her screaming through the phone.

Margaret chuckles.

ALEXANDER
She wants an update when you're pregnant. 

MARGARET
If. If I'm pregnant. 

ALEXANDER
She said when. I think it's when too.

Margaret looks at him gently, an element of uncertainty. 

MARGARET
I hope so.

CUT TO:

INT. FERTLITY CLINIC DOCTOR'S OFFICE - DAY

It's been 2 weeks. 

Margaret and Alexander sit in the office with Dr. Davies. 

MARGARET
Thank you! Thank you so much for all your help.

DR. DAVIES
I was more than happy to help - 
not everyone that comes in here are as nice as you two, or in my opinion, though you can't say this to anyone, Not everyone should be a parent, and many of the people I meet in this job are doing it for the wrong reason.

MARGARET
What reason is that? 

DR. DAVIES
Expectations from others, so they can fit into their families better, to give their life purpose...

Margaret gets lost on that last point "to give life purpose" 

DR. DAVIES
I know you two want a child because you know you are meant to be parents, and want to have a family. 

ALEXANDER
Well, thank you doc.

Margaret just nods, unsure how to answer.

EXT. FERTILITY CLINIC COURTYARD - DAY

Margaret and Alexander walk out of the fertility clinic. Margaret passes the stone statue of the woman. Margaret had never properly noticed her before. She stares for a second while walking, it feels like the statue stares back, but Margaret promptly forgets about it. 

CUT TO:

INT. WILLIAM'S HOUSE KITCHEN - NIGHT

It's been 2 weeks.  

Margaret has dinner cooking in the oven, while she sits and reads through an online blog from other mothers. She is whispering what she reads to herself even though she is the only person home. 

MARGARET (READING)
Many mothers will experience some form of postpartum depression after they give birth. 

MARGARET (SARCASTICALLY)
What about prepartum depression?

MARGARET (READING)
Prenatal depression is a form of depression that develops during pregnancy, and typically only lasts a few weeks.-I'm not pregnant enough for that. It's probably just nerves. 

Margaret stands up from the table. 

MARGARET
Right? Yep. That's probably it I'm just nervous. I'm ready to be a mother. I want to be a mother?

A bang against the window. Margaret jumps with shock, and moves towards the window. 

In the distance, a mysterious figure stands in the darkness, too difficult to make out. Margaret turns on the outside lights and the figure is gone.

Margaret shuts the kitchen curtain quickly out of fear. 

INT. WILLIAM'S HOUSE LIVING ROOM - NIGHT 

It's been a week since the woman appeared outside the window.

Margaret is sitting on the couch, searching through parenting forums. Alexander sits in an arm chair in the corner of the room reading. 

ALEXANDER
What do you think of the name, Edward if they're a boy?

MARGARET
Hmmm.

ALEXANDER
Is that really your response?

MARGARET
Sorry? What did you ask?

ALEXANDER
I asked if you liked the name Edward.

Margaret nods her head.

ALEXANDER
Are you ok? You seem distracted?

MARGARET
Yes, sorry I do like the name Edward.

Alexander looks at her with concern.

Beat. 

MARGARET
I'm fine. I'm just thinking about the baby alot and how were going to be when they arrive.

ALEXANDER
I think about that too. Let me grab you a tea, and we can talk more about the kid.

MARGARET
That sounds nice.

Alexander gets up from his chair and kisses Margaret on the forehead as he leaves the room. Margaret keeps scrolling online.

A bang on the window. Margaret jumps. She walks to the window, opening the side of the curtains. The stone statue from the fertility clinic stands outside the window. 

Margaret shrieks as she struggles to close the curtains back up.

Alexander runs back into the room.

ALEXANDER
WHAT'S WRONG!? IS SOMETHING OUTSIDE!?

Alexander rushes to the window and opens the curtains. They both look outside, the figure is gone.

MARGARET
Oh, sorry I thought I saw something, a person or something else.

ALEXANDER
REALLY!? I'll go look outside.

MARGARET
You don't need to do that; I. I think it's just the pregnancy brain; I'm just seeing stuff.

Alexander grabs her hands, and leads her to the couch.

ALEXANDER
Is everything alright? With you and the baby? 

Margaret looks ashamed. Like she has done something wrong.

ALEXANDER
You don't really want to talk about the pregnancy, and when you do it's about the pregnancy messing up your brain.

MARGARET
No. It's nothing

ALEXANDER
Because you've been falling asleep with your laptop open and as much as I try not to, I do worry about you. 

Margaret looks more ashamed than before.

ALEXANDER
And I saw the blogs - Are you worried about the baby?

MARGARET
I'm; I'm not unhappy. I just; there's this thought in my mind. Like I'm not ready to be a mother, and I think I'm just nervous about it, or I'm; I'm just scared.

ALEXANDER
I want you to tell me these things, because we're doing this together - and you can tell me anything.

MARGARET
I promise I will.

CUT TO:

INT. WILLIAM'S HOUSE BEDROOM - NIGHT

It's been 2 months.

Margaret sits on the bed, her stomach showing. She is surrounded by magazines of baby rooms, and is on the phone with Alexander.

ALEXANDER (O.S.)
Sorry that I wasn't home for dinner. 

MARGARET
It's absolutely fine. I know you're busy. It is getting late. 

ALEXANDER (O.S.)
I know. I will be home soon. Are you ok though?

MARGARET
Yes. I'm fine, just looking at designs for the babies' room. 

ALEXANDER
And that feels alright?

MARGARET
Hmmm. Yeah.

Alexander knows she's lying. Margaret knows he knows.

ALEXANDER
I'm just leaving the office now. I'll be home in about 20 minutes, love you. Bye!
  

Alexander hangs up on Margaret before she can say anything back. 

Margaret gets up and puts on her robe. 

INT. WILLIAM'S HOUSE KITCHEN - NIGHT

Margaret stands by the bench finishing making a cup of tea. She stares out of the window. 

As Margaret picks up her tea she looks down, as she looks back up, she notices something moving in the bushes. 

She hears a creak on the floorboards behind her, the stone statue is behind her in her kitchen. Margaret jumps in shock, dropping her mug and hot tea splashing everywhere. 

MARGARET (SCARED)
WHAT DO YOU WANT!?

THE STATUE
Take. 

MARGARET
TAKE WHAT!? 

The statue doesn't respond directly to the question, but points at her stomach.

THE STATUE
Take.

MARGARET
My baby?

THE STATUE
Take.

MARGARET
GET OUT! GET OUT! GET OUT!

Margaret falls to the ground leaning against the cupboard's screaming. 

INT. WILLIAM'S HOUSE ENTRY HALLWAY - NIGHT

Alexander bursts through the front door hearing Margaret's screaming and runs to her. 

INT. WILLIAM'S HOUSE KITCHEN - NIGHT

Margaret is on the floor eyes closed crying, talking faintly, her voice is going.

MARGARET
No!

ALEXANDER
WHAT'S WRONG!? 

Margaret shakes her head. Alexander rushes over to comfort her, holding her tight.

INT. WILLIAMS HOUSE LIVING ROOM - DAY

It's been 2 Month. 

Margaret sits on a couch opposite her therapist, Doctor May, an older woman, who is seated in the corner armchair. Doctor May has done a house call for Margaret today.

DR. MAY
Have you seen her since out session?

MARGARET
No. 

DR. MAY
Why do you think that is?

MARGARET
I - I'm not sure. Why do you think it is?

DR. MAY
You already know why I'm asking.

MARGARET
She is real. She feels real.

DR. MAY
If she is real, why do you think it's this particular statue. Why is it the one from the clinic?

MARGARET
I don't know.

DR. MAY
Do you think that she want's your baby?

MARGARET
Why would you say that!? 

Beat.

MARGARET
Is that really what you think she means by take?

DR. MAY
Maybe it could be. Why would she want to take your baby though?

MARGARET
I don't know. 

Beat. 

MARGARET
Maybe she knows something I don't know, or something?

DR. MAY
What would that be?

MARGARET
Maybe that I'm scared.

DR. MAY
Of motherhood?

MARGARET
Yes and no. I think I'm scared that if she wants to take the baby that it's because she knows; that she knows that I don't deserve the baby.

DR. MAY
Why wouldn't you deserve the baby.

MARGARET
Because.

Beat.

MARGARET
Because, I didn't, I couldn't conceive naturally. So maybe I don't deserve the baby, maybe I'm not meant to be a mother.

Dr. May nods and writes something in her notepad. Margaret is close to sobbing, but is holding it in.

MARGARET
I always wanted to be a mother, and when I found out that my body couldn't I started to think that maybe it just wasn't in the cards for me. Then, Alex's mother gave us the money for the IVF and then we became pregnant, and then I started to just think maybe I'm not meant to have the baby, like my body couldn't create it by itself so maybe I'm not meant to have it.  

DR. MAY
Do you think that the statue thinks it deserves the baby more than you?

MARGARET
I don't know. 

DR. MAY
Hypothetically, If the statue wasn't real. Why do you think your brain chose her.

MARGARET
Maybe because she's at the clinic. Maybe because she looked like she was meant to have a baby.

DR. MAY
What do you mean by that?

MARGARET
She looks maternal. Like she was meant to have a baby.

Dr. May writes another note down.

MARGARET
Do you think, that since it's a statue I sound more crazy?

DR MAY
No. I don't think you're crazy. I do think that since it's a statue is very interesting though. Maybe you relate to the statue. Since, like her, you couldn't have a baby without help, and maybe you related to her because of that. And maybe you have been feeling stuck like her statue and now you are free and you're scared of what that means going forward.

MARGARET
Do you really think that I projected my insecurities onto a statue? 

DR MAY
It doesn't so much matter what I think, but rather what you believe. I am glad you opened up to me about this.

Margaret smiles at her.

>CUT TO: 

EXT. FERTILITY CLINIC COURTYARD - EVENING

1 month later. 

Margaret sits on a bench near the statue, her stomach showing 4 months of pregnancy. Margaret stares at the statue, thinking over what to say or if to say anything. 

Margaret walks up to the statue.

MARGARET
Hello, again?

No response from the statue. 

MARGARET
Maybe, you're not alive. Or maybe you are. Either way, I want to thank you. Because you made me think about how I felt about this baby. I wasn't sure if I'm meant to be a mother, and I'm still not really sure. But I'm excited to be one. And whether it was actually you or just in my head. I want to thank you. My therapist suggested that we are alike, like we have both been statues in different ways, or a very similar way. So I wanted to give you something

Beat.

Margaret takes out a toy doll from her bag and places it in the arms of the statue.

MARGARET
I hope it makes you as happy as my baby will make me. 

Margaret walks away. 

The statue sits, holding her baby closely. 
